From: SoCKit-IDS project <ids@sockit.local>
Subject: [PATCH] fpga: bridge: make state attribute writable

Userspace needs to gate AXI traffic across the HPS<->FPGA bridge during
runtime FPGA reconfiguration.  Without it, writing a new bitstream
while a process holds an mmap of the LW bridge faults the HPS (the
fabric is in reset for ~100 ms during write_init/write/write_complete,
but the bridge is still "enabled" and any AXI transaction during that
window panics the kernel).

The fpga_bridge_enable() / fpga_bridge_disable() functions already
exist; this just plumbs them through to a writable sysfs attribute.
Same change landed upstream in later kernels; backporting onto
rel_socfpga-5.11.

Accepted values:
    "enabled"   -> fpga_bridge_enable()
    "disabled"  -> fpga_bridge_disable()
    "1"         -> fpga_bridge_enable()
    "0"         -> fpga_bridge_disable()
Returns -EINVAL on anything else.

Signed-off-by: SoCKit-IDS project <ids@sockit.local>
---
--- a/drivers/fpga/fpga-bridge.c
+++ b/drivers/fpga/fpga-bridge.c
@@ -301,8 +301,31 @@
 	return scnprintf(buf, 10, "%s\n", enable ? "enabled" : "disabled");
 }
 
+static ssize_t state_store(struct device *dev,
+			   struct device_attribute *attr,
+			   const char *buf, size_t count)
+{
+	struct fpga_bridge *bridge = to_fpga_bridge(dev);
+	bool enable;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (sysfs_streq(buf, "enabled") || sysfs_streq(buf, "1"))
+		enable = true;
+	else if (sysfs_streq(buf, "disabled") || sysfs_streq(buf, "0"))
+		enable = false;
+	else
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	if (enable)
+		ret = fpga_bridge_enable(bridge);
+	else
+		ret = fpga_bridge_disable(bridge);
+
+	return ret ? ret : count;
+}
+
 static DEVICE_ATTR_RO(name);
-static DEVICE_ATTR_RO(state);
+static DEVICE_ATTR_RW(state);
 
 static struct attribute *fpga_bridge_attrs[] = {
 	&dev_attr_name.attr,
